{#
This host's own endpoints, as a Prometheus file_sd fragment.

Written here, on the host, rather than assembled on the control node,
because the host is where the answer is authoritative: these are the
ports its unit files actually render, from the same facts that rendered
them. playbooks/metrics-targets.yml only collects the fragments and
concatenates them, so the control node never has to recompute a plan --
or hold the LDAP group memberships it would need to compute one.

One object per transport mount, because that is the granularity of an
rclone metrics server: one process, one mount, one endpoint (see
metrics_ports() in filter_plugins/stortree.py).

Targets prefer the non-loopback listeners, so a scraper elsewhere gets
an address it can actually reach. A loopback-only host still gets its
endpoints listed -- they are reachable from an agent running on the host
itself, or through an SSH tunnel -- rather than silently emitting
nothing, which would look identical to metrics being switched off.

`stortree_slug` is a label rather than a nicety: it is the one string an
alert has to carry for the person woken by it to run `systemctl status
stortree-remote@<slug>`, and it is not derivable from the path by eye
(slugs are systemd-escaped -- see _slug()).
#}
{% set scrape = stortree_metrics_listeners | rejectattr('loopback') | list %}
{% set rows = [] %}
{% for entry in stortree_mounts_plan | selectattr('kind', 'equalto', 'transport') | sort(attribute='local_path') %}
{%   set port = stortree_metrics_ports[entry.slug] %}
{%   set addresses = [] %}
{%   for listener in (scrape if scrape else stortree_metrics_listeners) %}
{%     set _ = addresses.append(listener.listen ~ ':' ~ port) %}
{%   endfor %}
{%   set _ = rows.append({
         'targets': addresses,
         'labels': {
           'stortree_host': inventory_hostname,
           'stortree_node': entry.local_path,
           'stortree_slug': entry.slug,
           'stortree_remote': entry.remote,
         },
       }) %}
{% endfor %}
{{ rows | to_nice_json }}
